WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:03.240
And let's have a quick look again to our bronze layer specifications.

00:03.240 --> 00:07.160
So we just have to load the data from the sources to the data warehouse.

00:07.160 --> 00:09.520
We're going to build tables in the bronze layer.

00:09.520 --> 00:10.960
We are doing a full load.

00:10.960 --> 00:13.840
So that means we are truncating and then inserting the data.

00:13.880 --> 00:17.080
There will be no data transformations at all in the bronze layer.

00:17.080 --> 00:20.000
And as well we will not be creating any data model.

00:20.000 --> 00:23.040
So this is the specifications of the bronze layer.

00:23.080 --> 00:23.400
All right.

00:23.440 --> 00:28.520
Now in order to create the DDL script for the bronze layer, creating the tables of the bronze, we

00:28.520 --> 00:33.080
have to understand the metadata, the structure, the schema of the incoming data.

00:33.080 --> 00:38.000
And here either you ask the technical experts from the source system about these informations, or you

00:38.000 --> 00:43.280
can go and explore the incoming data and try to define the structure of your tables.

00:43.280 --> 00:44.120
So now what we're going to do.

00:44.120 --> 00:46.920
We're going to start with the first source system the CRM.

00:47.080 --> 00:48.600
So let's go inside it.

00:48.600 --> 00:51.440
And we're going to start with the first table the customer info.

00:51.480 --> 00:55.600
Now if you open the file and check the data inside it, you see we have a header information.

00:55.600 --> 01:01.120
And that is very good because now we have the names of the columns that are coming from the source and

01:01.120 --> 01:01.840
from the content.

01:01.840 --> 01:04.240
You can define of course the data types.

01:04.240 --> 01:05.280
So let's go and do that.

01:05.280 --> 01:07.320
First we're going to say create table.

01:07.320 --> 01:09.420
And then we have to define the layer.

01:09.420 --> 01:10.420
It's going to be the bronze.

01:10.460 --> 01:13.100
And now very important we have to follow the naming convention.

01:13.100 --> 01:15.420
So we start with the name of the source system.

01:15.420 --> 01:17.380
It is CRM underscore.

01:17.380 --> 01:20.260
And then after that the table name from the source system.

01:20.260 --> 01:22.860
So it's going to be the cost underscore info.

01:22.860 --> 01:25.980
So this is the name of our first table in the bronze layer.

01:26.020 --> 01:29.340
Then the next step we have to go and define of course the columns.

01:29.340 --> 01:34.900
And here again the column names in the bronze layer are going to be 1 to 1 exactly like the source system.

01:34.900 --> 01:36.740
So the first one is going to be the ID.

01:36.980 --> 01:39.020
And I will go with the data type integer.

01:39.020 --> 01:43.780
Then the next one going to be the key and varchar and the length.

01:43.780 --> 01:45.300
And we'll go with 50.

01:52.180 --> 01:55.140
And the last one going to be the create dates.

01:55.180 --> 01:56.340
It's going to be a date.

01:56.340 --> 02:00.420
So with that we have covered all the columns available from the source system.

02:00.420 --> 02:01.420
So let's go and check.

02:01.420 --> 02:03.700
And yes the last one is the create date.

02:03.700 --> 02:05.180
So that's it for the first table.

02:05.220 --> 02:08.740
Now semicolon of course at the end let's go and execute it.

02:08.780 --> 02:11.860
And now we're going to go to the Object Explorer over here refresh.

02:11.860 --> 02:15.320
And we can see the first table inside our data warehouse.

02:15.360 --> 02:16.360
Amazing, right?

02:16.400 --> 02:23.120
So now next, what you have to do is to go and create a DDL statement for each file for those two systems.

02:23.200 --> 02:26.080
So for the CRM we need three DLLs.

02:26.120 --> 02:32.120
And as well for the other system the ERP, we have as well to create three DLLs for the three files.

02:32.120 --> 02:36.800
So at the ends we can have in the bronze layer six tables six Dtls.

02:36.800 --> 02:39.120
So now pause the video, go create those details.

02:39.120 --> 02:41.960
I will be doing the same as well and we will see you soon.

02:47.440 --> 02:47.760
All right.

02:47.760 --> 02:49.800
So now I hope you have created all those details.

02:49.800 --> 02:51.760
I'm going to show you what I have just created.

02:51.760 --> 02:57.720
So the second table in the source CRM, we have the product informations and the third one is the sales

02:57.720 --> 02:58.440
details.

02:58.920 --> 03:00.560
Then we go to the second system.

03:00.560 --> 03:03.040
And here we make sure that we are following the naming convention.

03:03.040 --> 03:06.920
So first the source system Erb and then the table name.

03:06.920 --> 03:08.960
So the second system was really easy.

03:09.000 --> 03:11.120
You can see we have only here like two columns.

03:11.120 --> 03:13.240
And for the customers like only three.

03:13.280 --> 03:15.600
And for the categories only four columns.

03:15.640 --> 03:16.040
All right.

03:16.040 --> 03:19.520
So after defining those stuff of course we have to go and execute them.

03:19.520 --> 03:21.240
So let's go and do that.

03:21.280 --> 03:25.020
And then we go to the object explorer over here, refresh the tables.

03:25.020 --> 03:29.500
And with that you can see we have six empty tables in the bronze layer.

03:29.500 --> 03:33.900
And with that we have all the tables from the two source systems inside our database.

03:33.900 --> 03:35.820
But still we don't have any data.

03:35.820 --> 03:38.460
And you can see our naming convention is really nice.

03:38.460 --> 03:44.020
You see the first three tables comes from the CRM source system, and then the other three comes from

03:44.020 --> 03:44.740
the IRB.

03:44.780 --> 03:48.340
So we can see in the bronze layer that things are really splitted nicely.

03:48.340 --> 03:52.460
And you can identify quickly which table belongs to which source system.

03:52.460 --> 03:58.500
Now there is something else that I usually add to the DDL script is to check whether the table exists

03:58.500 --> 03:59.740
before creating.

03:59.740 --> 04:04.380
So for example, let's say that you are renaming or you would like to change the data type of specific

04:04.380 --> 04:04.780
field.

04:04.780 --> 04:09.700
If you just go and run this query, you will get an error because the database is going to say we have

04:09.700 --> 04:10.780
already this table.

04:10.780 --> 04:14.060
So in other databases you can say create or replace table.

04:14.060 --> 04:17.660
But in the SQL server you have to go and build a T-sql logic.

04:17.660 --> 04:18.780
So it is very simple.

04:18.820 --> 04:22.100
First we have to go and check whether the object exists in the database.

04:22.100 --> 04:27.420
So we say if object ID and then we have to go and specify the table name.

04:27.420 --> 04:34.320
So let's go and copy the whole thing over here and make sure you get exactly the same name as the table

04:34.320 --> 04:34.520
name.

04:34.520 --> 04:35.600
So there is like space.

04:35.600 --> 04:36.960
I'm just going to go and remove it.

04:36.960 --> 04:39.440
And then we're going to go and define the object type.

04:39.440 --> 04:41.720
So it's going to be the you it stands for user.

04:41.720 --> 04:43.680
It is the user defined tables.

04:43.680 --> 04:46.120
So if this table is not null.

04:46.400 --> 04:49.560
So that means the database did find this object in the database.

04:49.560 --> 04:50.760
So what can happen.

04:50.760 --> 04:54.160
We say go and drop that table.

04:54.160 --> 04:56.920
So the whole thing again and semicolon.

04:56.920 --> 05:02.480
So again if the table exists in the database is not null then go and drop the table.

05:02.480 --> 05:04.520
And after that go and create it.

05:04.520 --> 05:09.600
So now if you go and highlight the whole thing and then execute it, it will be working.

05:09.600 --> 05:11.160
So first drop the table.

05:11.160 --> 05:14.880
If it exists then go and create the table from scratch.

05:14.880 --> 05:20.760
Now what you have to do is to go and add this check before creating any table inside our database.

05:20.760 --> 05:23.240
So it's going to be the same thing for the next table.

05:23.240 --> 05:24.040
And so on.

05:24.040 --> 05:27.440
I went and added all those checks for each table.

05:27.720 --> 05:31.200
And what can happen if I go and execute the whole thing?

05:31.240 --> 05:32.040
It's going to work.

05:32.040 --> 05:36.600
So with that, I'm recreating all the tables in the bronze layer from the scratch.
